data codex_structured_error_code_authentication_required: NonEmptyStr = "authentication_required" as NonEmptyStr

data codex_account_union_note: String = "GetAccountResponse.account IS A CLOSED DISCRIMINATED UNION, not an opaque object. Read from `codex app-server generate-json-schema --out` against @openai/codex 0.146.0 — the version pinned by gunbc.package_delivery codex_required_package_version and materialized by the committed provider-runtime/codex lock — v2/GetAccountResponse.json: Account is a oneOf over ApiKeyAccount \{type:'apiKey'\}, ChatgptAccount \{type:'chatgpt', planType, email\} and AmazonBedrockAccount \{type:'amazonBedrock', usesCodexManagedCredentials\}, discriminated by the required `type` member. `account` itself is nullable and, unlike requiresOpenaiAuth, is NOT in the response's required list — an absent account and a null account are both reachable.\n\nTHESE ARE THREE DIFFERENT CREDENTIAL REALIZATIONS, not three spellings of one account. An apiKey account bills a platform key; an amazonBedrock account bills a cloud contract; only chatgpt is the personal OAuth subscription. A consumer whose profile requires personal ChatGPT OAuth is not satisfied by the other two, so decoding this member to a bare `the object was nonempty` discards the only fact that distinguishes them.\n\nplanType is REQUIRED on ChatgptAccount. A chatgpt account without one is a malformed frame, not an account on an unnamed plan."

data codex_plan_type_note: String = "PlanType IS A CLOSED ENUM of exactly 13 values, cited from the same v2/GetAccountResponse.json definitions block at 0.146.0. It is enumerated here rather than kept as an open string because upstream closes it, and modeling a closed upstream vocabulary as an open one is the anemic leaf §2 names.\n\nTHE LOAD-BEARING MEMBER IS `unknown`. It is a real enum value, so the wire can carry a plan whose identity upstream itself does not assert. Treating any decoded planType as a positive entitlement signal would therefore read upstream's own I-do-not-know as a yes — the fabricated-plausible-output failure with an authoritative-looking source. `unknown` decodes successfully and entitles nothing.\n\nA plan string OUTSIDE this enum is a different state again: upstream added a plan this pin does not know. That is CodexPlanTypeUnrecognized, carrying the native token, never silently folded into CodexPlanUnknown — the first says our model is stale, the second says upstream is uncertain, and the remedies differ.\n\nONE RUNTIME PRODUCES ALL PROTOCOL FACTS. This enum was first authored with 15 members read from a 0.147.0 executable that happened to be installed on the build host, while every other fact in this module — and the materialized bundle the turn actually executes — is 0.146.0. Measured, the two differ: 0.147.0 adds self_serve_business_prolite and enterprise_cbp_automation, so the modeled interface described plans the pinned executable cannot emit and no exact runtime implemented the composite. The account union is structurally identical across both, which is precisely why the defect was easy to miss — the part that changed was the part nobody diffed.\n\nThe rule the correction establishes is not which version wins. It is that a protocol fact is cited from the exact executable the runtime receipt admits; a version bump moves the manifest, lock, admitted artifacts, runtime receipt and every fact in this module together, or it does not happen. Under 0.146.0 the two 0.147.0-only plan strings decode as CodexPlanTypeUnrecognized, which is the honest reading — a bumped runtime would then surface them as a stale pin rather than silently widening the model."

type CodexAccountType
  = CodexAccountTypeApiKey
  | CodexAccountTypeChatgpt
  | CodexAccountTypeAmazonBedrock

fn codex_account_type_wire_name(t: CodexAccountType) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match t {
    CodexAccountTypeApiKey => "apiKey" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexAccountTypeChatgpt => "chatgpt" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexAccountTypeAmazonBedrock => "amazonBedrock" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

type CodexPlanType
  = CodexPlanFree
  | CodexPlanGo
  | CodexPlanPlus
  | CodexPlanPro
  | CodexPlanProlite
  | CodexPlanTeam
  | CodexPlanSelfServeBusinessUsageBased
  | CodexPlanBusiness
  | CodexPlanEnt26
  | CodexPlanEnterpriseCbpUsageBased
  | CodexPlanEnterprise
  | CodexPlanEdu
  | CodexPlanUnknown

fn codex_plan_type_wire_name(p: CodexPlanType) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match p {
    CodexPlanFree => "free" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanGo => "go" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanPlus => "plus" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanPro => "pro" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanProlite => "prolite" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanTeam => "team" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanSelfServeBusinessUsageBased => "self_serve_business_usage_based" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanBusiness => "business" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanEnt26 => "ent26" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanEnterpriseCbpUsageBased => "enterprise_cbp_usage_based" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanEnterprise => "enterprise" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanEdu => "edu" as NonEmptyStr
    CodexPlanUnknown => "unknown" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data codex_account_type_key: NonEmptyStr = "type" as NonEmptyStr
data codex_account_plan_type_key: NonEmptyStr = "planType" as NonEmptyStr

data codex_rate_limits_capacity_decode_note: String = "GetAccountRateLimitsResponse carries rateLimits (backward-compatible single-bucket mirror) AND rateLimitsByLimitId (multi-bucket map keyed by limit_id, e.g. codex vs codex_bengalfox). Live exhausted Pro accounts can have codex at usedPercent 100 with rateLimitReachedType rate_limit_reached while another limit_id remains at 0 — a single account-level Ready|QuotaReached is therefore false. Decode every by-limit-id entry; keep native limit tokens open. spendControlReached true is an exhaustion signal when present, but live exhaustion often has spendControlReached false — usedPercent 100 and rateLimitReachedType are the operative facts. Empty rateLimits:\{\} answers the endpoint without proving capacity."

data codex_rate_limit_subject_encoding_note: String = "Standing bridge subject encoding (coord with gunbc.provider_standing / #7926, deep-raven-684 2026-08-06): each rateLimitsByLimitId entry projects to ProviderNativeLimitBucket \{ native_key: limit_id \} — NEVER ProviderAccountLimit. Promoting an exhausted codex bucket to account scope would block for the wrong reason, erase bucket identity, and wrongly refuse work that a sibling bucket (e.g. codex_bengalfox) could serve. If a truthful native-bucket observation does not block a dispatch that draws on that bucket, the defect is in limit_observation_blocks_selection coverage, not in this encoding. Discriminating control from the live capture: one inventory with codex at 100 percent and codex_bengalfox at 0 percent must refuse a dispatch that draws on codex and allow a dispatch that draws on codex_bengalfox."

data codex_model_to_limit_bucket_gap_note: String = "TYPED GAP — model → native limit_id is NOT observable on the 0.146 app-server surface. Model has no limitId; TurnStartParams.model is a free string with no bucket edge; RateLimitSnapshot carries limitId/limitName but no model join. Live limitName GPT-5.3-Codex-Spark on codex_bengalfox is suggestive folklore, not a cited join. dissolve-on: an upstream field or documented map that binds turn model (or service tier) to metered limit_id. Until then, consumers must not invent a closed mapping. The symbol codex_default_rate_limit_id names the backward-compatible rateLimits mirror's native key (live limitId=codex), which is the press AccountReady gate key — not a proven claim that every unspecified-model turn/start consumes that bucket."

